> So anyway, what tips can you give me?  Should I always play Gnomes and
> head for Minetown asap to do the protection racket?  

As a gnome, the Mines are less dangerous. Even if you're not racketeering,
picking up a point or five of extra AC never hurts. I would at least
do the first level of the mines to get some armor off of dwarves (and
hopefully some daggers; daggers at Skilled are still pretty good, and
you need anything that counts as "pretty good" as a Healer).

I can (apparently, checking logs) get four out of five to Minetown at XL
3 or less, but the low XL seems to hurt me even after I get the AC; both
of my most successful Healer games (choked on a wraith corpse post-Quest
with full kit, and ascended) I got there at XL 3, not XL 1. Your mileage
may vary, though (and people on RGRN will argue ad infinitum given the
chance :). I recommend trying for XL 3 or XL 4 but haven't played fifty
games to test this and certainly haven't been fifty players.

> When is it safe to ditch the pet, or should I keep it? 

I don't ditch the pet, but when it dies and I don't want a new one, I
start pacifying domestic animals instead of taming them. I can always
come back and tame them later, and the same tin works every time.

This usually happens to me after:

 * Minetown, where the pet can score me armor off of the Watch and
   maybe a silver saber
 * Sokoban, where if nothing else I use the pets for the zoo
 * I have a reasonable weapon

If I have a magic whistle, I'll keep playing with pets until I am kitted.
Also if I manage to polymorph a pet into something good, I'll keep it longer.
Just be careful about them going untame --- I once had a Vampire Lord with 
AC -25 kicking around while I was still XL 8. I could not hit him for tens
of thousands of turns...
 
> What is a good weapon to replace the scapel with before I get the Staff? 

Grayswandir! :) 

If you have a coaligned altar or unicorn access for bumping your luck,
either a mattock or a silver saber is pretty awesome. Unicorn horn also
works well, and doesn't require the additional encumbrance of a weapon,
but they can be hard to come by. Usually I end up with an elven dagger
that doesn't stack and is hopefully positively enchanted, though, rather
than any of those options, even if they are theoretically superior.

Also, I don't find skill slots that important for healers. If you get
an artifact (you could get Magicbane!), use it and #enhance it, unless
it's seriously no better than what you have. Hell, even Vorpal Blade
isn't bad; you won't need to pray for food, consider getting crowned 
early if the Minetown altar is coaligned. Again, easier said than done,
but since what you will want to do depends on what the RNG gives you,
I figured I'd mention a few things that have worked for me.

> Should I quaff those potions in the beginning or save them?

Many RGRN veterans tell you to quaff them. I prefer saving them ---
you'll still get the max HP boost when you drink them in a bind, and
that's a few more early binds you can get out of. I'm usually more
worried about getting hit 3 times for 4 damage than once for 15. But,
as always, use your judgement. "If healing or extra healing is smoky,
you may want to think harder"

Hope these suggestions help. Once you get past the early game, Healers
are fun; ascending with tame Famine and Pestilence makes up for all the
rest of it, in my opinion.

